1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,470
If trading is free, who pays?

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:07,950
Every day, millions of people open a
trading app, tap buy and pay nothing.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,436
No commission, no fee, zero dollars.

4
00:00:11,486 --> 00:00:15,736
And yet, the companies behind
those apps are worth billions.

5
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:19,353
So, if trading is free, who's paying?

6
00:00:19,663 --> 00:00:24,093
To find out, we're going to follow one
single trade from the moment you tap

7
00:00:24,143 --> 00:00:26,813
that button to a firm you've
probably never heard of,

8
00:00:26,863 --> 00:00:29,354
sitting right in the
middle of Wall Street.

9
00:00:29,424 --> 00:00:31,454
This is Billions and Bulls.

10
00:00:31,504 --> 00:00:33,834
Let's follow the money.

11
00:00:34,837 --> 00:00:40,547
Part 1 The $0 Trade When you tap buy,
your order usually doesn't go straight

12
00:00:40,597 --> 00:00:41,794
to the stock exchange.

13
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:45,234
It goes to a market maker, a
firm like Citadel Securities,

14
00:00:45,284 --> 00:00:48,514
that pays your broker
for the right to fill it.

15
00:00:48,564 --> 00:00:51,190
It's called payment for order flow.

16
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,070
The market maker earns a tiny slice of
the spread, the gap between what buyers

17
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:58,930
pay and what sellers receive.

18
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:03,099
Fractions of a cent per share,
millions of times a day.

19
00:01:03,329 --> 00:01:05,039
Here's what that looks like.

20
00:01:05,089 --> 00:01:08,539
Imagine a stock where
buyers are bidding $99.98

21
00:01:09,249 --> 00:01:11,819
and sellers are asking $100.02.

22
00:01:12,223 --> 00:01:15,053
That four cent gap is the spread.

23
00:01:15,103 --> 00:01:16,973
A market maker stands in the middle.

24
00:01:17,023 --> 00:01:21,879
It buys from the sellers, sells to
the buyers, and keeps the difference.

25
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:26,139
To win your order, it will often give
you a slightly better price than the one

26
00:01:26,189 --> 00:01:29,000
on your screen. Maybe $100.01

27
00:01:29,070 --> 00:01:30,919
instead of $100.02.

28
00:01:31,229 --> 00:01:34,635
You save a penny. The market
maker keeps the rest.

29
00:01:34,685 --> 00:01:39,195
Minus a small cut, it pays your
broker for sending you there.

30
00:01:39,245 --> 00:01:41,794
Now multiply that by millions of orders.

31
00:01:41,844 --> 00:01:47,074
In October 2025, Robinhood sent its
customer stock orders to six of these firms

32
00:01:47,124 --> 00:01:52,689
Virtu, G1, Jane Street, Citadel Securities,
Hudson River Trading and Two Sigma.

33
00:01:52,739 --> 00:01:56,609
every single one of them paid for the
privilege, together about forty million

34
00:01:56,659 --> 00:01:59,149
dollars in one month.

35
00:01:59,699 --> 00:02:02,243
But stocks are the smaller
part of this story.

36
00:02:02,293 --> 00:02:05,203
The real money is somewhere
most beginners never look,

37
00:02:05,253 --> 00:02:07,583
and we'll get there.

38
00:02:08,053 --> 00:02:12,587
Part 2 The Firm in the Middle First,
meet the biggest of these firms.

39
00:02:12,637 --> 00:02:17,627
Citadel Securities says it handles more
than 35% of all retail trading volume

40
00:02:17,677 --> 00:02:20,827
in the United States and
about 30% of all U.S.

41
00:02:20,877 --> 00:02:22,860
listed options trading.

42
00:02:22,929 --> 00:02:29,119
In the first three months of 2025 alone,
it reportedly made a record $3.4 billion

43
00:02:29,169 --> 00:02:30,943
in trading revenue.

44
00:02:31,173 --> 00:02:35,283
So why would a firm that big pay for
orders from people buying three shares

45
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:40,060
at a time? Because your order is one
of the safest orders in the market.

46
00:02:40,110 --> 00:02:44,060
When a giant hedge fund starts buying,
it might be the first slice of a huge

47
00:02:44,110 --> 00:02:47,660
position, and the price is about to
move against whoever sold to it.

48
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:50,445
Your order is just your order.

49
00:02:50,514 --> 00:02:55,004
Small, random, and unlikely to
know something the market doesn't.

50
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:56,784
Think of it like car insurance.

51
00:02:56,834 --> 00:03:01,275
Insurers love careful drivers because
they rarely cost them anything.

52
00:03:01,325 --> 00:03:05,915
To a market maker, retail orders are
the careful drivers of the stock market.

53
00:03:05,965 --> 00:03:07,595
That's the best flow there is.

54
00:03:07,645 --> 00:03:10,705
So, it pays for the right
to trade against it.

55
00:03:10,934 --> 00:03:15,764
And that creates the conflict critics
point to: your broker is being paid by

56
00:03:15,814 --> 00:03:18,475
the firm on the other side of your trade.

57
00:03:18,625 --> 00:03:23,375
In December 2020, the SEC said Robinhood
had misled customers about exactly

58
00:03:23,425 --> 00:03:28,662
this. Between 2015 and 2018, According
to the SEC Robinhood's customers got

59
00:03:28,712 --> 00:03:32,182
worse prices, costing them thirty
four point one million dollars,

60
00:03:32,232 --> 00:03:34,022
even after the commissions they saved.

61
00:03:34,072 --> 00:03:36,806
Robinhood paid sixty five
million dollars to settle.

62
00:03:36,856 --> 00:03:39,905
without admitting or denying the findings.

63
00:03:40,605 --> 00:03:43,835
Part 3: Where the real money is.

64
00:03:43,885 --> 00:03:47,275
Now, remember that place
most beginners never look?

65
00:03:47,325 --> 00:03:49,004
Look at Robinhood's own numbers.

66
00:03:49,054 --> 00:03:54,284
In the last three months of 2025,
stock trading brought in $94 million.

67
00:03:54,334 --> 00:03:56,524
Options brought in $314

68
00:03:56,574 --> 00:03:59,725
million, more than three times as much.

69
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:03,985
Options are contracts that let you bet
on where a stock will be by a certain

70
00:04:04,035 --> 00:04:08,305
date. They're complex, they move
fast, and people trade them often.

71
00:04:08,355 --> 00:04:11,196
Each contract usually covers 100 shares.

72
00:04:11,246 --> 00:04:15,016
and market makers pay
Robinhood for every contract.

73
00:04:15,326 --> 00:04:19,436
In October twenty twenty five alone,
market makers paid Robinhood more than

74
00:04:19,486 --> 00:04:22,747
fifty million dollars for its
customers' options orders,

75
00:04:22,797 --> 00:04:26,347
About four in every ten of
those orders went to one firm,

76
00:04:26,397 --> 00:04:28,487
Citadel Securities.

77
00:04:28,877 --> 00:04:33,157
Crypto added another $221
million in that same quarter.

78
00:04:33,207 --> 00:04:35,397
And the fastest growing line of all?

79
00:04:35,447 --> 00:04:37,237
Bets on real world events.

80
00:04:37,287 --> 00:04:39,557
Robinhood calls them prediction markets.

81
00:04:39,607 --> 00:04:43,725
Customers traded eight point five billion
event contracts in those three months.

82
00:04:43,775 --> 00:04:46,845
and the revenue line that includes them
grew more than three hundred percent

83
00:04:46,895 --> 00:04:50,205
in a year, and on these
Robinhood charges a commission,

84
00:04:50,255 --> 00:04:52,745
up to a cent per contract.

85
00:04:53,055 --> 00:04:54,528
The pattern is simple.

86
00:04:54,578 --> 00:04:57,408
The more complex the product
and the more often you trade it,

87
00:04:57,458 --> 00:05:00,668
the more money someone
makes from your order.

88
00:05:00,818 --> 00:05:03,568
Quick thing: if you want more
videos that follow the money,

89
00:05:03,618 --> 00:05:08,378
subscribe. It's free, and
unlike trading, It actually is

90
00:05:09,008 --> 00:05:11,758
Part 4: The Money You Forgot About.

91
00:05:11,808 --> 00:05:13,111
But here's the twist.

92
00:05:13,161 --> 00:05:18,071
In the last three months of 2025, Robinhood's
single biggest revenue line wasn't

93
00:05:18,121 --> 00:05:20,791
options or crypto or stocks.

94
00:05:20,841 --> 00:05:26,468
It was interest. four hundred
and eleven million dollars of it

95
00:05:26,518 --> 00:05:29,188
Out of every dollar Robinhood
made in those three months,

96
00:05:29,238 --> 00:05:31,471
about thirty two cents came from interest.

97
00:05:31,521 --> 00:05:35,711
25 from options, 17 from
crypto, 11 from other trading,

98
00:05:35,761 --> 00:05:40,979
like prediction markets, only 7 from
stocks, and 8 from everything else.

99
00:05:41,209 --> 00:05:43,640
That interest comes from three places.

100
00:05:43,690 --> 00:05:46,999
One, the cash customers
leave sitting uninvested.

101
00:05:47,049 --> 00:05:50,359
Robinhood sweeps it to partner
banks, earns interest on it,

102
00:05:50,409 --> 00:05:52,586
and passes only part of that on.

103
00:05:52,636 --> 00:05:57,386
two. Margin. Customers borrow money to
trade and pay interest on the loan.

104
00:05:57,436 --> 00:05:59,306
three. Securities lending.

105
00:05:59,356 --> 00:06:03,306
Robinhood lends out shares to traders
who want to bet against a stock and earns

106
00:06:03,356 --> 00:06:06,161
a fee. Your idle cash isn't idle.

107
00:06:06,211 --> 00:06:09,550
It's working, just not entirely for you.

108
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:15,650
Add it all up, and in 2025 Robinhood
brought in $4.5 billion in revenue and

109
00:06:15,700 --> 00:06:18,246
kept $1.9 billion as profit.

110
00:06:18,296 --> 00:06:22,086
It now has twenty seven
million funded customers.

111
00:06:22,136 --> 00:06:25,766
By Robinhood's own math, it makes about
a hundred and ninety one dollars a

112
00:06:25,816 --> 00:06:30,068
year from the average one, from
an app that's supposedly free.

113
00:06:30,378 --> 00:06:35,048
And 4.2 million of those customers now
pay for a subscription called Robinhood

114
00:06:35,098 --> 00:06:36,548
Gold.

115
00:06:37,397 --> 00:06:42,787
Part 5 The Day It Broke Free trading
works beautifully until everyone trades

116
00:06:42,837 --> 00:06:44,607
at once.

117
00:06:44,677 --> 00:06:47,063
January 28th, 2021

118
00:06:47,113 --> 00:06:51,303
GameStop, a struggling video game retailer,
had gone from two dollars and fifty

119
00:06:51,353 --> 00:06:55,143
seven cents a share in April twenty
twenty to as high as four hundred eighty

120
00:06:55,193 --> 00:06:56,663
three dollars that day.

121
00:06:56,713 --> 00:07:00,936
Millions of people were piling
in, many of them on Robinhood.

122
00:07:00,986 --> 00:07:04,136
Then Robinhood did something
its users never expected.

123
00:07:04,186 --> 00:07:05,736
It stopped them from buying.

124
00:07:05,786 --> 00:07:08,506
They could sell. They just couldn't buy.

125
00:07:08,556 --> 00:07:11,846
That day, GameStop fell 44%.

126
00:07:12,507 --> 00:07:16,137
The Internet exploded with
theories, but the explanation,

127
00:07:16,187 --> 00:07:17,417
later laid out by a U.S.

128
00:07:17,467 --> 00:07:21,077
House committee, was more
boring and more revealing.

129
00:07:21,446 --> 00:07:24,756
Back then, a stock trade
took two days to settle.

130
00:07:24,806 --> 00:07:28,676
To cover the risk in between, brokers
post collateral at a clearinghouse called

131
00:07:28,726 --> 00:07:30,039
the NSCC.

132
00:07:30,089 --> 00:07:35,559
As the trading went wild, the NSCC
demanded $3.7 billion in collateral from

133
00:07:35,609 --> 00:07:37,107
Robinhood.

134
00:07:37,337 --> 00:07:41,687
Robinhood agreed to restrict buying in
13 stocks, and the demand was cut to

135
00:07:41,737 --> 00:07:46,817
about 1.4 billion. Within days it raised
three point four billion dollars from

136
00:07:46,867 --> 00:07:49,377
its investors to make
sure it could keep up.

137
00:07:49,427 --> 00:07:53,777
Three weeks later, Robinhood's CEO
was testifying before Congress.

138
00:07:53,827 --> 00:07:56,988
So was Ken Griffin, The head of Citadel.

139
00:07:57,299 --> 00:08:01,009
For a few days, millions of people saw
the plumbing behind the free button.

140
00:08:01,059 --> 00:08:04,188
It ran on billions of
dollars in collateral.

141
00:08:05,002 --> 00:08:07,992
Part 6. So, is free trading a scam?

142
00:08:08,042 --> 00:08:10,181
No, not exactly.

143
00:08:10,251 --> 00:08:16,421
In 2019, before the big brokers
dropped to zero, Schwab charged $4.95

144
00:08:16,731 --> 00:08:18,347
for an online stock trade.

145
00:08:18,397 --> 00:08:22,087
TD Ameritrade and E-Trade charged $6.95.

146
00:08:22,877 --> 00:08:26,039
Today, that same trade
costs nothing upfront.

147
00:08:26,089 --> 00:08:30,039
And the industry argues market makers
often give you a better price than the

148
00:08:30,089 --> 00:08:32,039
public, quote.

149
00:08:32,089 --> 00:08:33,819
But not everyone is convinced.

150
00:08:33,869 --> 00:08:37,739
In the United Kingdom, payment for order
flow has effectively been banned since

151
00:08:37,789 --> 00:08:41,019
twenty twelve. The European
Union banned it too.

152
00:08:41,069 --> 00:08:45,231
Its last exemption in Germany
ended in june twenty twenty six.

153
00:08:45,281 --> 00:08:49,952
In the US, the SEC proposed sending
retail orders into open auctions,

154
00:08:50,002 --> 00:08:53,451
then withdrew that proposal in June 2025.

155
00:08:54,241 --> 00:08:56,423
And it's not only about prices.

156
00:08:56,473 --> 00:09:01,303
In twenty twenty, Massachusetts regulators
accused Robinhood of treating trading

157
00:09:01,353 --> 00:09:05,543
like a game, with confetti raining
down on the screen after trades.

158
00:09:05,593 --> 00:09:08,306
Robinhood dropped the confetti
in twenty twenty one,

159
00:09:08,356 --> 00:09:14,306
In 2024, it paid $7.5
million to settle the case.

160
00:09:14,356 --> 00:09:17,377
So, if trading is free, who's paying?

161
00:09:17,607 --> 00:09:21,317
Partly the market makers who pay for
your orders because they make money on

162
00:09:21,367 --> 00:09:25,877
them, and partly you, through the spread,
through the interest on cash you forgot

163
00:09:25,927 --> 00:09:30,567
about. And through every extra trade
you make because it feels like it costs

164
00:09:30,617 --> 00:09:32,067
nothing.

165
00:09:32,137 --> 00:09:35,987
The price isn't zero, it's
just not on the receipt.

166
00:09:36,624 --> 00:09:40,255
Next time, we follow the money
behind the card in your wallet,

167
00:09:40,305 --> 00:09:43,592
who really gets paid
every time you tap to pay.

168
00:09:43,642 --> 00:09:46,392
And if you want the other side
of the economy, the ships,

169
00:09:46,442 --> 00:09:50,472
planes and rockets behind everything
you buy, check out our sister channel,

170
00:09:50,522 --> 00:09:52,612
Cargo and Capital.

171
